# Canonical author identities.
#
# Git records whatever name/email was configured on the machine that made the
# commit. Over this repo's life a few wrong or stale identities got recorded.
# This file maps them to the right person WITHOUT rewriting history, so
# `git log`, `git shortlog`, `git blame` and GitHub's contributor graph all show
# one entry per human.
#
# Format:  Correct Name <correct@email>  Wrong Name <wrong@email>
#
# WHY NOT REWRITE HISTORY INSTEAD:
# The earliest wrong-identity commit is 535f939 (2025-10-01). Rewriting it means
# rewriting all 358 commits after it, including 143 of Bob's and 35 of Peter's,
# and force-pushing. Bob committed 3 days before this file was written, so his
# clone (and everyone else's) would break. Not worth it to correct a name.

# Wrong identity: a different company's address, recorded because the global git
# config pointed at it before this repo got a local override. 21 commits,
# Oct 2025 to Jan 2026. Global config has since been corrected.
luka <lukahood@paymenthood.com> amirhAva <Abdolmanaf@avainfotech.co>

# Same person, older addresses from before the paymenthood.com address existed
# and from the shared VpnHood theme lineage.
luka <lukahood@paymenthood.com> Luka <luka@paymenthood.com>
luka <lukahood@paymenthood.com> luka <luka@vpnhood.com>
luka <lukahood@paymenthood.com> Luka <luka@vpnhood.com>

# Same person, two spellings.
Peter <peter@vpnhood.com> petervhood <peter@vpnhood.com>

# Same person, two addresses.
Bob <bob@paymenthood.com> Bob <bob@vpnhood.com>
